Pipe A fills the tank in 30 minutes, so its rate is \( \frac{1}{30} \) tank/minute.  
Pipe B fills the tank in 20 minutes, so its rate is \( \frac{1}{20} \) tank/minute.  
Pipe C fills the tank in 10 minutes, so its rate is \( \frac{1}{10} \) tank/minute.  

When all three are opened together for 3 minutes, the fraction of the tank filled by each is:  
A: \( 3 \times \frac{1}{30} = \frac{3}{30} = \frac{1}{10} \)  
B: \( 3 \times \frac{1}{20} = \frac{3}{20} \)  
C: \( 3 \times \frac{1}{10} = \frac{3}{10} \)  

Total tank filled in 3 minutes:  
\[
\frac{1}{10} + \frac{3}{20} + \frac{3}{10} 
= \frac{2}{20} + \frac{3}{20} + \frac{6}{20} 
= \frac{11}{20}
\]

Pipe C discharges solution r, so the proportion of solution r in the liquid after 3 minutes is:  
\[
\frac{\text{Part from C}}{\text{Total filled}} 
= \frac{\frac{3}{10}}{\frac{11}{20}} 
= \frac{3}{10} \times \frac{20}{11} 
= \frac{60}{110} 
= \frac{6}{11}
\]

